          "id": 2980247088,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "The comment in bd7c6bda88272183dfbbf5fe146ccc638a864cd9, which introduced the `select()` call in the capture path, says\n\n```\n   /*\n    * Sigh.  The semantics of the read timeout argument to\n    * \"pcap_open_live()\" aren't particularly well specified by\n    * the \"pcap\" man page - at least with the BSD BPF code, the\n    * intent appears to be, at least in part, a way of cutting\n    * down the number of reads done on a capture, by blocking\n    * until the buffer fills or a timer expires - and the Linux\n    * libpcap doesn't actually support it, so we can't use it\n    * to break out of the \"pcap_dispatch()\" every 1/4 of a second\n    * or so.\n    *\n    * Thus, on Linux, we do a \"select()\" on the file descriptor for the\n    * capture, with a timeout of CAP_READ_TIMEOUT milliseconds, or\n    * CAP_READ_TIMEOUT*1000 microseconds.\n    */\n```\n\n*If* the expectation in our code is that a `pcap_dispatch()` call will be guaranteed to return within 250ms, libpcap doesn't guarantee that this works (it's better documented now, but the documentation says \"it depends on the device on which you're capturing).\n\nCapture mechanisms other than the OS's network device capture mechanism, such as Linux's usbmon, may not provide timeouts, either (usbmon doesn't).",
          "author": {
            "id": 5636682,
            "username": "guyharris",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Guy Harris",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
