        {
          "id": 3031739345,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "But I don't think those other capture mechanisms will, if packets arrive only rarely, indefinitely hold up those packets waiting for a full buffer.\n\nA comment before `capture_loop_dispatch()` in current dumpcap says\n\n```\n/* dispatch incoming packets (pcap or capture pipe)\n *\n * Waits for incoming packets to be available, and calls pcap_dispatch()\n * to cause them to be processed.\n *  \n * Returns the number of packets which were processed.\n *\n * Times out (returning zero) after CAP_READ_TIMEOUT ms; this ensures that the\n * packet-batching behaviour does not cause packets to get held back\n * indefinitely.\n */\n```\n\nThe last paragraph suggests that the problem is with the capture mechanism potentially holding onto packets for an indefinite period of time in the hope that the buffer will fill up Real Soon Now. Only capture mechanisms that don't deliver packets immediately have that problem, and if they have a timeout that's guaranteed to expire, at a minimum, some short amount of time after the first packet arrives, they don't have that problem.\n\nSo:\n\n- BPF has buffering to return packet batches, and has timeouts that expire a specified amount of time after a `read()` is done - but that are not guaranteed to expire a specified amount of time after a `select()` is done (fixed in most \\*BSDs, including CupertinoBSD, in sufficiently-recent releases, but not fixed yet in OpenBSD, and may not be fixed in Solaris 11/Illumos or AIX);\n- Linux `PF_PACKET` sockets, when you're just receiving messages on the socket, don't have timeouts but also do no buffering;\n- Linux `PF_PACKET` sockets, when you're using `TPACKET_V3`, has buffering *and* has timeouts (some kernel versions have botches in that, but libpcap works around them) - the timeouts do *no* expire on an empty buffer;\n- WinPcap/Npcap have buffering and has timeouts that prevent packets from being buffered indefinitely - I haven't checked whether they time out on an empty buffer or not;\n- the DLPI capture buffering mechanism in Solaris (not used in Solaris 11, which has BPF) has buffering and a timeout, and the timeout doesn't, as I remember, trigger unless at least one packet has been received;\n- the HP-UX DLPI capture mechanism has no buffering mechanism, so packet arrive as they arrive, and there's no timeout;\n- Haiku (yes, there's both an official libpcap module and a Wireshark port) has `PF_LINK` sockets, with, it appears, no timeout and no buffering;\n- the GNU HURD (yes, there's an official libpcap module in the main branch) gets packets, one at a time, via Mach messages, with no receive timeout and, as far as I know, no buffering;\n- other OSes are dead at this point.\n\nAs for other capture mechanisms:\n\n- from what I can tell, the DAG card driver uses a timeout as the way to block until packets arrive, and the card doesn't notify the host of packets arriving, so you get batching and a timeout;\n- I'm not sure what the heck happens with DPDK;\n- I think the Myricom SNF stuff also has batching and a timeout;\n- for netmap, most of the packet-receiving loop is done in the netmap library, and if it gets no packets, it blocks with a `poll()` on the netmap FD, with a timeout, so it appears to be batching+timeout;\n- for D-Bus, it receives a message at a time, with a wired-in 100ms timeout, and no buffering;\n\netc.\n\nThis is a bit of a mess. Non-blocking mode isn't always supported, timeouts aren't always supported, timeouts may or may not occur if no packets have arrived (the main purpose of timeouts is to make sure that, if there are packets waiting to be read, they don't get stuck indefinitely in the buffer if the buffer doesn't fill up), etc.\n\nSo this requires 1) some further thought, 2) some cleanup work in libpcap, and 3) figuring out what to do about `pcap_breakloop()` if it's not called as a result of a signal that will cause a call that waits for a packet to arrive to be interrupted.",
