      "diff": "@@ -231,14 +231,17 @@ static void capture_loop_stop(void);\n /** Close a pipe, or socket if \\a from_socket is true */\n static void cap_pipe_close(int pipe_fd, bool from_socket);\n \n-#if defined (__linux__)\n-/* whatever the deal with pcap_breakloop, linux doesn't support timeouts\n- * in pcap_dispatch(); on the other hand, select() works just fine there.\n- * Hence we use a select for that come what may.\n+#if defined (__linux__) && !defined(HAVE_PCAP_INIT)\n+/* pcap_init was introduced in libpcap 1.10, which also requires PF_PACKET\n+ * support and kernel 2.6.27 or later, so at least TPACKET_V2. TPACKET_V3\n+ * supports timeouts directly, and for TPACKET_V1 and TPACKET_V2 libpcap\n+ * supports timeouts using poll() internally.\n  *\n- * XXX - with TPACKET_V1 and TPACKET_V2, it currently uses select()\n- * internally, and, with TPACKET_V3, once that's supported, it'll\n- * support timeouts, at least as I understand the way the code works.\n+ * However, with earlier libpcap versions, it's possible to compile without\n+ * PF_PACKET support, or for the library to fall back to SOCK_PACKET sockets\n+ * if the running kernel doesn't support them. Linux doesn't support timeouts\n+ * in pcap_dispatch in that case; on the other hand select() works just fine.\n+ * Hence on earlier versions we use a select come what may.\n  */\n #define MUST_DO_SELECT\n #endif\n",
