      "title": "dumpcap: Don't define MUST_DO_SELECT on Linux with libpcap 1.10",
      "message": "dumpcap: Don't define MUST_DO_SELECT on Linux with libpcap 1.10\n\nOn Linux, libpcap did not support timeouts with the old (pre-kernel 2.2)\nSOCK_PACKET sockets. With memory-mapped packet capture on PF_PACKET\nsockets, timeouts are supported. With TPACKET_V1 (kernel 2.2) and TPACKET_V2\n(kernel 2.6.27) libpcap implements timeouts internally with poll(), and\nTPACKET_V3 (kernel 3.2) supports timeouts itself.\n\nEven though all those are quite old now (and libpcap has supported\nTPACKET_V3 since 1.5.0, our minimum required version), up until libpcap\n1.10.0 it was possible to build libpcap without memory-mapped packet\ncapture support and thus timeout support on Linux (including via a\nconfiguration option disabling it.) As of 1.10.0, PF_PACKET and\nTPACKET_V2 or later must be supported for libpcap to compile.\n\nFor supporting SOCK_PACKET sockets, dumpcap uses select itself since\npcap_dispatch does not support timeouts. Check for pcap_init, which\nbecame available in libpcap 1.10.0, and if we have that, don't define\nMUST_DO_SELECT and instead rely upon libpcap handling the timeouts.\n",
      "author_name": "John Thacker",
